} {"question" "q-353387" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi +8"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a94c35f3004d8b5604ac96ab132510459a314aa5cac913813d0a431abaf75a3" "basis" 1266 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-432" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^5 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\\,?$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3, & x<0 \\\\ 2x+3, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/example-432.json" "file_sha256" "56e94a6e43f7791b16ce88d775bd5d24166385feb3298658bf6fba5ab6cec906" "basis" 1266 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-432" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}3\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{5}(2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3x{|}_{-2}^{0}+({x}^{2}+3x){|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =[3(0)-3(-2)]+[({5}^{2}+3(5))-({0}^{2}+3(0))] \\\\  & =6+25+15 \\\\  & =46.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/example-432.json" "file_sha256" "56e94a6e43f7791b16ce88d775bd5d24166385feb3298658bf6fba5ab6cec906" "basis" 1266 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-433" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/ad45cbdcd126df931f1a35c60ca12b45d520a2a0a5cddf4fa012c0515d3024c7.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/example-433.json" "file_sha256" "c83c7637a9688f7b373c3effa8693da82a332617a4df56e089bd7963d894bb44" "basis" 1266 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-433" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x \\in [0,2)$ and the line segment for $x \\in [2,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = \\underbrace{\\int_{0}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{A_1} + \\underbrace{\\int_{2}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{-A_2}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [0,2).$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =bh \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [2,4].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n\\int_{2}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x =-A_2 = -2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2} \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/example-433.json" "file_sha256" "c83c7637a9688f7b373c3effa8693da82a332617a4df56e089bd7963d894bb44" "basis" 1266 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-434" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/36c6a1c84e0b0c32a5fd35fd422645f60340da6d205dd8cc31054cd558c2f5f9.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-4}^4 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/example-434.json" "file_sha256" "9265f0acf258503c6032c37b255ea2b1425b9d43803fc4bd6e02a8f526100f50" "basis" 1266 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-434" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of four parts: the arc of a semi-circle for $x\\in [-4,0],$ a line segment for $x\\in (0,2),$ a line segment for $x\\in (2,4],$ and the point at $x=2.$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+0+{{{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [-4,0].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle with a radius of $2,$ so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in (0,2).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = -A_2 = -2.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in (2,4].$  The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\pi -2+2 \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/example-434.json" "file_sha256" "9265f0acf258503c6032c37b255ea2b1425b9d43803fc4bd6e02a8f526100f50" "basis" 1266 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-5046" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n${\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}4, & x<0 \\\\ -x+4, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-5046" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}4\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{4}(-x+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4x{|}_{-3}^{0}+(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+4x){|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =[4(0)-4(-3)]+[-\\frac{(4)^{2}}{2}+4(4)+\\frac{(0)^{2}}{2}-4(0)] \\\\  & =[0+12]+[-8+16+0-0] \\\\  & =12+8 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-5046" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "814df2abe04b250e428078ed6ec87b6f3a4822e775d32a8a038cb87b48178c81"} {"question" "q-5046" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-5046" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "20"] ["math" "28"] ["math" "36"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-5046" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "20"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-341981" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x-5, & x<2, \\\\ 1, & x\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-341981" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}x-5)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{2}^{6}1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-5x){|}_{-4}^{2}\\,+\\,x{|}_{2}^{6} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{4}(2)^{2}-5(2)\\,-\\,(\\frac{1}{4}(-4)^{2}-5(-4))]\\,+\\,[\\,6-2\\,] \\\\  & =[1-10\\,-\\,(4+20)]+4 \\\\  & =[-9-24]+4 \\\\  & =-33+4 \\\\  & =-29.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-341981" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "814df2abe04b250e428078ed6ec87b6f3a4822e775d32a8a038cb87b48178c81"} {"question" "q-341981" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-341981" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-29"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-342004" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-x+4, & x<1, \\\\ 2x-3, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-342004" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}(-x+4)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{1}^{4}(2x-3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+4x){|}_{-3}^{1}+({x}^{2}-3x){|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =[\\,-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+4\\cdot 1-(-\\frac{(-3)^{2}}{2}+4(-3))]+\\,[{4}^{2}-3\\cdot 4-({1}^{2}-3\\cdot 1)] \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{2}+4-(-\\frac{9}{2}-12)]+[16-12-(1-3)] \\\\  & =[\\frac{7}{2}-(-\\frac{33}{2})]+6 \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{2}+6 \\\\  & =20+6 \\\\  & =26.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-342004" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "814df2abe04b250e428078ed6ec87b6f3a4822e775d32a8a038cb87b48178c81"} {"question" "q-342004" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-342004" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "26"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-29739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n${\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x<1 \\\\ 6-5x, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-29739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}(6-5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})|}_{0}^{1}+{(6x-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{2})|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{(1)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(0)^{3}}{3}]+(6(2)-\\frac{5(2)^{2}}{2}-6(1)+\\frac{5(1)^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}]+[12-10-6+\\frac{5}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-29739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "814df2abe04b250e428078ed6ec87b6f3a4822e775d32a8a038cb87b48178c81"} {"question" "q-29739" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-29739" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{6}{7}"] ["math" "-\\frac{7}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{7}"] ["math" "\\frac{6}{7}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-29739" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{7}{6}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-5047" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n${\\int }_{-5}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+2, & x<1 \\\\ 3, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-5047" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-5}^{1}(x+2)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{3}3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2x){|}_{-5}^{1}+3x{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{(1)^{2}}{2}+2(1)-\\frac{(-5)^{2}}{2}-2(-5)]+[3(3)-3(1)] \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}+2-\\frac{25}{2}+10]+[9-3] \\\\  & =0+6 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-5047" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "814df2abe04b250e428078ed6ec87b6f3a4822e775d32a8a038cb87b48178c81"} {"question" "q-5047" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-5047" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "18"] ["math" "24"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-5047" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-18004" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/assets/d43fa0efcfb8788316c86958b1175741c136588e81a8119131d1ca0f8d8ee229.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of four line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-18004" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of four parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,-2),$ the line segment for $x\\in [-2,-1),$ the line segment for $x\\in [-1,1),$ and the line segments for $x\\in [1,3].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{4}}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,-2).$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,-1).$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-1.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,1).$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,3].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{4}=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}-{A}_{4} \\\\  & =1-1+2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-18004" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "814df2abe04b250e428078ed6ec87b6f3a4822e775d32a8a038cb87b48178c81"} {"question" "q-18004" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-18004" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-18004" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-316122" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/assets/64ce1ae5f198ce41e55414d853b1bd99e73d747cddb80412b30246bfdb13a907.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-316122" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,1)$ and the line segment for $x\\in (1,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,= & {\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,= & \\,{{{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}\\,+\\,{{{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,1).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (1,4].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\, & =\\,{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\,8+6 \\\\  & =\\,14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-316122" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "814df2abe04b250e428078ed6ec87b6f3a4822e775d32a8a038cb87b48178c81"} {"question" "q-316122" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-316122" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "14"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-338319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/assets/82096496503c058ae31473029f7844ddff99fad024dd786c7bad88985279a9af.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-5}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-338319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-5,-1)$ and the line segment for $x\\in (-1,2].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,= & {\\int }_{-5}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+{\\int }_{-1}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{-1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {\\int }_{-5}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,= & \\,{{{\\int }_{-5}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}\\,+\\,{{{\\int }_{-1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-5,-1).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-1,2].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =7.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\, & =\\,{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\,6+7.5 \\\\  & =\\,13.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-338319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "814df2abe04b250e428078ed6ec87b6f3a4822e775d32a8a038cb87b48178c81"} {"question" "q-338319" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-338319" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{27}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-338505" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/assets/a1a419a984000b3b8559a19809416bcccc9eb8341985994c621c3a48d15109da.png)\n\nThe graph $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of three line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-338505" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We divide the interval of integration into $4$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-5,-4],$ the line segment for $x\\in [-4,-3),$ the line segment for $x\\in [-3,0],$ and the line segment for $x\\in (0,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-5}^{-4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-4}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{4}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-5,-4].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-3).$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,0].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (0,4].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =bh \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}+{A}_{4} \\\\  & =1-1+\\frac{9}{2}+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-338505" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "814df2abe04b250e428078ed6ec87b6f3a4822e775d32a8a038cb87b48178c81"} {"question" "q-338505" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-338505" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{17}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-117680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/assets/a433e9e9f2018c1f964f13b0ef248f72253f99f071f44e029c928598e9d04a32.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-117680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,0)$ and the line segment for $x\\in [0,2].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,0).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,2].$ The corresponding region is a square, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =8+4 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-117680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "814df2abe04b250e428078ed6ec87b6f3a4822e775d32a8a038cb87b48178c81"} {"question" "q-117680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-117680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "18"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-117680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-49215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/assets/11cf2a28b6ce6281de0299a4a585bc3c98bf891f6d958401f22aab2fc6ce5ef2.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of ${\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-49215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of three parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,-2],$ the arc of a semi-circle for $x\\in (-2,2],$ and the line segment for $x\\in (2,3].$  So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe first integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-2].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =bh \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-2,2].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle of radius $2.$ Thus, we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe third integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (2,3].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid. Thus, we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+4)(1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =6-2\\pi +3 \\\\  & =9-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-49215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "814df2abe04b250e428078ed6ec87b6f3a4822e775d32a8a038cb87b48178c81"} {"question" "q-49215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-49215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2+3\\pi "] ["math" "11+\\pi "] ["math" "7+2\\pi "] ["math" "8-\\frac{\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "9-2\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-49215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9-2\\pi "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-339677" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/assets/353412402972b390de950f56b3273d8dbecf60d4d07c7c27e9281c28145a86d2.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-339677" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of $4$ parts: the arc of the quarter-circle for $x\\in [-3,-1],$ the line segment for $x\\in [-1,1],$ the line segment for $x\\in (1,4],$ and the arc of the quarter-circle for $x\\in [4,7].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{4}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{4}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,-1].$ The corresponding region is a combination of a rectangle and a quarter-circle of radius $2,$ so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =bh-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =(2)(3)-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi (2)^{2} \\\\  & =6-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=\\pi -6.\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,1].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =bh \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (1,4].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+3)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [4,7].$ The corresponding region is a quarter-circle of radius $3,$ so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi (3)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}+{A}_{4} \\\\  & =\\pi -6-2+6+\\frac{9\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{13\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\pi -8}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a90186