x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,3).$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =b\\cdot h \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,5].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot (1)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =10+\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-49176" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "814df2abe04b250e428078ed6ec87b6f3a4822e775d32a8a038cb87b48178c81"} {"question" "q-49176" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-49176" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10+\\frac{3\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "10+\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "10+\\pi "] ["math" "5+\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "5+\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-49176" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10+\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-49169" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/assets/555b702c1903f8cc4e379ad4281aa86708757755e4f9fb14b0ec7790a80a17dd.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-49169" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of $3$ parts: the arc of the quarter-circle for $x\\in [-4,-2),$ the arc of the quarter-circle for $x\\in (-2,0],$ and the line segment for $x\\in (0,4].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-2}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-2).$ The corresponding region is a quarter-circle with the radius of $2$, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-2,0].$ The corresponding region is a quarter-circle with the radius of $2$, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (0,4].$  The corresponding region is the trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (2+4)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{3}=-12.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\pi +\\pi -12=2\\pi -12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "0712847810c27833ad998a692a9b442f498624a9018603ea5a7300aefb761f8e"} {"question" "q-49169" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "814df2abe04b250e428078ed6ec87b6f3a4822e775d32a8a038cb87b48178c81"} {"question" "q-49169" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/state.json" "file_sha256" "